  m_Name: BackwardCurvedCentrifugalFanData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Backward Curved Centrifugal Fan
  engineDescription: A Backward Curved Centrifugal Fan is a dynamic fluid-flow machine
    used to move air or gases by converting mechanical energy from a rotating impeller
    into pressure and kinetic energy. Its main components include the impeller, backward-curved
    blades, casing, shaft, motor, inlet, outlet, bearings, and supporting structure.
    During operation, air enters axially through the fan inlet and is accelerated
    outward by the rotating impeller. The backward-curved blades direct the air toward
    the outer circumference while reducing the amount of energy required for a given
    airflow. The surrounding casing collects the high-velocity air and converts part
    of its kinetic energy into static pressure before directing it toward the outlet.
    These fans are widely used in HVAC systems, industrial ventilation, air-handling
    units, dust collection systems, and process industries. From an engineering perspective,
    understanding fan pressure, airflow rate, blade geometry, system resistance,
    efficiency, power consumption, noise, vibration, and operating-point selection
    is essential for reliable and energy-efficient performance.
  engineCategory: Engineering
